nge over N periods (usually 14) — the real volatility a name breathes on a normal day, independent of direction. It's directly useful the moment you place GOOG's stop today: a flat \"5% below entry\" is arbitrary — on a name with a 1.5% ATR, 5% is three days of normal noise away and won't get hit by chop; on a 6%-ATR name, 5% is inside a single day's range and gets stopped on nothing. Better rule: 1.5–2× ATR below entry, not a round percentage. It's also the volatility-adjusted version of what your exit-rules file already says — derive stops from real support/resistance, not arbitrary numbers.\n                  </p>\n                  <p style=\"margin: 12px 0 0 0; font-size: 12px; color: #475569; font-style: italic;\">\n                    Also today: <strong>Chandelier exit</strong> — a trailing stop set at N×ATR below the highest high since entry; tightens automatically as a position runs, without you touching the order.\n                  </p>\n                </td>\n              </tr>\n            </table>\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n\n        <!-- FOOTER -->\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 16px 24px 24px 24px; border-top: 1px solid #f3f4f6;\">\n            <p style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 10px; color: #9ca3af; line-height: 1.6;\">\n              Research output, not financial advice. Past performance does not guarantee future results.<br>\n              Prices: Yahoo Finance JSON API (query1 + query2) and stooq.com were all blocked by this session's network egress proxy — no live prices fetched today, same issue as yesterday's run. GOOG figures above use the last confirmed price ($353.65, Aug 9 via IBKR).<br>\n              portfolio.json: last_updated 2026-08-05T12:00:00+03:00 — stale AND inaccurate (missing the GOOG position, wrong cash figure), unchanged since flagged in yesterday's report. Every figure here is reconciled against the Aug 9 IBKR snapshot instead; nothing is live-verified for today. Confirm directly in IBKR before acting.